1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,600
Take a large number.

2
00:00:03,050 --> 00:00:04,850
For example, ten thousand.

3
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:07,500
What is the chance that it is prime?

4
00:00:07,950 --> 00:00:10,150
Well, you probably answered 0.

5
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:14,200
I asked a silly question,
so I got a silly answer.

6
00:00:14,650 --> 00:00:16,450
Ten thousand is not prime.

7
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:18,700
So let me try again.

8
00:00:19,150 --> 00:00:22,350
Take a random number around ten thousand:

9
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:24,800
what is the chance that it is prime?

10
00:00:25,250 --> 00:00:28,050
Well, that's not well-posed either.

11
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:32,100
What does "around" mean?
And what does "random" mean?

12
00:00:32,550 --> 00:00:34,750
So let's define it more precisely.

13
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:41,000
We draw a number at most one percent
of ten thousand away from it,

14
00:00:41,250 --> 00:00:46,650
and we do it so that all these numbers
are equally likely to be chosen.

15
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:50,700
We ask:
what is the probability that it is prime?

16
00:00:51,691 --> 00:00:54,891
We have a computer,
so this is not a hard question.

17
00:00:55,341 --> 00:00:59,941
We just need to count how many
primes there are in this range

18
00:01:00,091 --> 00:01:02,209
and divide by the size of the range.

19
00:01:02,932 --> 00:01:04,732
Well, we got an answer.

20
00:01:05,182 --> 00:01:08,782
This wasn't a very
informative question either...

21
00:01:09,232 --> 00:01:13,232
We asked about a specific number
and got a specific answer.

22
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:17,940
Let's ask this question for many numbers.

23
00:01:18,390 --> 00:01:21,590
Here too,
it's not hard to do with a computer.

24
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:24,440
We run over all the numbers in this range,

25
00:01:24,690 --> 00:01:29,546
and for each one we compute the share
of primes within one percent of it.

26
00:01:30,273 --> 00:01:35,473
We get a graph of the chance that a
number within one percent of n is prime,

27
00:01:35,723 --> 00:01:36,799
as a function of n.

28
00:01:37,249 --> 00:01:40,249
Let's call this function p₁%(n).

29
00:01:40,699 --> 00:01:43,899
We don't expect an exact formula for it,

30
00:01:44,149 --> 00:01:49,404
just as we don't expect an
informative formula for the primes.

31
00:01:49,854 --> 00:01:53,254
But we can try to
understand how it behaves.

32
00:01:54,040 --> 00:01:56,640
We see in the graph
that it is quite small.

33
00:01:57,090 --> 00:02:00,490
It's a bit hard to see
things about small numbers.

34
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:04,340
So instead we'll talk about 1/p₁%(n).

35
00:02:04,790 --> 00:02:07,790
Let's call this function r₁%(n),

36
00:02:08,840 --> 00:02:12,640
the "rarity" of the primes around n.

37
00:02:13,090 --> 00:02:17,290
We see that this function increases,
but rather slowly.

38
00:02:18,088 --> 00:02:20,288
Let's switch to a logarithmic scale.

39
00:02:21,138 --> 00:02:23,338
We see a clear pattern here:

40
00:02:23,788 --> 00:02:28,188
to a very good approximation,
it is a linear function of the logarithm.

41
00:02:28,638 --> 00:02:30,379
Let's check which one.

42
00:02:31,102 --> 00:02:37,902
Again, a computer shows that the natural
logarithm approximates r₁%(n) well,

43
00:02:38,152 --> 00:02:40,162
at least in the range
where we can compute.

44
00:02:40,896 --> 00:02:44,096
The choice of one percent
was rather arbitrary.

45
00:02:44,546 --> 00:02:48,146
We can choose any other percentage
and get a similar result.

46
00:02:48,596 --> 00:02:51,096
A 5% window

47
00:02:51,246 --> 00:02:53,729
A 20% window

48
00:02:54,458 --> 00:02:56,258
This looks great on the graph,

49
00:02:56,508 --> 00:03:01,708
but how do we know it is a real
phenomenon that goes on to infinity,

50
00:03:01,958 --> 00:03:05,958
and not something that only looks right in
the picture and in the range we checked?

51
00:03:06,408 --> 00:03:08,608
How do we prove it?

52
00:03:09,058 --> 00:03:13,458
And come to think of it,
what on earth do we need to prove here?

53
00:03:13,908 --> 00:03:17,108
Well, the last question
is fairly easy to answer.

54
00:03:17,558 --> 00:03:21,558
We want to compare our
function with ln(n).

55
00:03:22,008 --> 00:03:26,208
One way to do this is to
divide one by the other,

56
00:03:26,458 --> 00:03:30,671
and we want to say that this
ratio is very close to 1.

57
00:03:31,121 --> 00:03:36,121
Of course, when n is small,
our statistics are not very good yet,

58
00:03:36,371 --> 00:03:38,171
and there can be a deviation.

59
00:03:38,621 --> 00:03:42,021
But as n grows,
the deviation tends to vanish.

60
00:03:42,471 --> 00:03:45,071
That's what limits were invented for.

61
00:03:45,521 --> 00:03:48,921
So our claim is really
that the limit is 1.

62
00:03:49,371 --> 00:03:52,571
And as we said,
any other percentage works too.

63
00:03:54,167 --> 00:03:58,967
Gauss and Legendre conjectured this,
in a slightly different form,

64
00:03:59,617 --> 00:04:02,617
and Hadamard and de la
Vallée Poussin proved it.

65
00:04:03,067 --> 00:04:06,067
It is called the Prime Number Theorem,

66
00:04:06,317 --> 00:04:08,388
and it is the subject of this video.

67
00:04:08,838 --> 00:04:13,438
Before the full proof,
Chebyshev proved a weaker version,

68
00:04:13,688 --> 00:04:16,288
bounding the ratio from above and below

69
00:04:16,538 --> 00:04:18,538
but the limit is not found,

70
00:04:18,788 --> 00:04:21,788
and it fails for percentages
that are too small.

71
00:04:22,238 --> 00:04:26,838
Later, Riemann developed a revolutionary
strategy for such problems.

72
00:04:29,088 --> 00:04:36,075
This strategy finally solved the problem,
and led to much fascinating mathematics.

73
00:04:36,812 --> 00:04:39,612
We won't prove the theorem now.

74
00:04:40,062 --> 00:04:44,062
Nor will we tell you about
Riemann's revolutionary ideas.

75
00:04:44,512 --> 00:04:47,512
We won't even explain Chebyshev's proof,

76
00:04:47,762 --> 00:04:49,562
although it isn't complicated.

77
00:04:50,012 --> 00:04:52,612
Instead, we'll prove a theorem of Mertens

78
00:04:52,862 --> 00:04:54,662
which doesn't imply it,

79
00:04:54,912 --> 00:04:57,252
but certainly makes a convincing
case that it should be true.

80
00:05:00,362 --> 00:05:03,362
Let's look at the number n!

81
00:05:03,812 --> 00:05:06,012
and try to factor it.

82
00:05:06,462 --> 00:05:09,262
How many times does 2 appear in it?

83
00:05:09,712 --> 00:05:11,512
That's fairly easy:

84
00:05:11,962 --> 00:05:14,562
once for every even number up to n,

85
00:05:14,812 --> 00:05:18,212
once more for every number divisible by 4,

86
00:05:18,662 --> 00:05:20,462
and so on.

87
00:05:20,912 --> 00:05:23,512
What about 3? It's similar,

88
00:05:23,762 --> 00:05:26,362
and so on for all the primes up to n.

89
00:05:26,812 --> 00:05:29,012
Writing it this way is cumbersome,

90
00:05:29,262 --> 00:05:31,862
so we'll use product and sum notation.

91
00:05:33,307 --> 00:05:36,707
These are quite large numbers,
so let's take logs.

92
00:05:37,157 --> 00:05:39,557
The log of a product is a sum of logs.

93
00:05:40,007 --> 00:05:42,807
We also know how to
take the log of a power.

94
00:05:43,257 --> 00:05:47,457
These integer parts are rather annoying,
so let's drop them.

95
00:05:47,707 --> 00:05:49,507
That will give us an error term,

96
00:05:49,757 --> 00:05:52,157
so the equality is no longer exact.

97
00:05:52,607 --> 00:05:55,807
Now we can factor out n.

98
00:05:56,257 --> 00:05:58,457
We are left with geometric series.

99
00:05:58,907 --> 00:06:02,107
You surely remember the
geometric series formula.

100
00:06:02,557 --> 00:06:06,157
We got n times the series of ln p/(p−1).

101
00:06:06,607 --> 00:06:10,007
Nothing will happen if
we replace p−1 with p.

102
00:06:10,457 --> 00:06:12,457
We're approximating anyway.

103
00:06:14,050 --> 00:06:15,850
What about the other side?

104
00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:19,900
A factorial is a product,
so again its log is a sum.

105
00:06:20,350 --> 00:06:22,950
This sum is not so easy to compute,

106
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:25,200
but we can approximate it well.

107
00:06:25,650 --> 00:06:30,050
The point is that for 99%
of the numbers up to n

108
00:06:30,300 --> 00:06:31,611
the log is roughly log n.

109
00:06:32,335 --> 00:06:36,135
So, quite accurately,
we can replace it with n ln(n).

110
00:06:36,585 --> 00:06:38,585
Now we can cancel n.

111
00:06:39,035 --> 00:06:44,635
We got an inexact formula for the
sum of ln p/p over the primes.

112
00:06:45,085 --> 00:06:48,885
And if we work hard enough,
we can bound its error.

113
00:06:49,335 --> 00:06:51,135
This is Mertens' theorem.

114
00:06:52,493 --> 00:06:56,293
But how is this related to
the Prime Number Theorem?

115
00:06:56,743 --> 00:06:58,943
Let's go back to the inexact form.

116
00:06:59,393 --> 00:07:01,193
There's an ln(n) here.

117
00:07:01,643 --> 00:07:04,643
You surely know another series like this.

118
00:07:05,093 --> 00:07:06,893
It's the harmonic series.

119
00:07:07,343 --> 00:07:09,143
So what did we get?

120
00:07:09,593 --> 00:07:11,993
So summing over all natural numbers

121
00:07:12,243 --> 00:07:15,043
is like summing over the primes only,

122
00:07:15,293 --> 00:07:17,323
but giving each prime p the weight ln(p).

123
00:07:17,773 --> 00:07:24,173
As if the primes around p were ln(p)
times sparser than the integers,

124
00:07:24,423 --> 00:07:29,200
and so they have to be counted with more
weight to balance their contribution.

125
00:07:29,650 --> 00:07:33,761
But this is exactly what the
Prime Number Theorem says.

126
00:07:34,211 --> 00:07:38,611
Well, this doesn't really
prove the Prime Number Theorem,

127
00:07:38,861 --> 00:07:41,261
since it only tells us about averages.

128
00:07:41,711 --> 00:07:46,111
There could be regions with
significantly fewer primes,

129
00:07:46,361 --> 00:07:48,372
and regions with significantly more.

130
00:07:48,822 --> 00:07:50,822
But it gives us a sense of it.

131
00:07:51,272 --> 00:07:57,672
In fact, it yields the following weak
version of the Prime Number Theorem:

132
00:07:58,471 --> 00:08:02,071
If the limit in the Prime
Number Theorem exists,

133
00:08:02,321 --> 00:08:03,909
then it equals 1.

134
00:08:04,359 --> 00:08:06,159
Why is this true?

135
00:08:06,609 --> 00:08:11,009
Because if the limit is not 1,
say it equals a number a,

136
00:08:11,259 --> 00:08:16,859
it easily follows that the harmonic
series is about a times Mertens' series,

137
00:08:17,109 --> 00:08:17,280
contradicting Mertens' theorem.

138
00:08:20,059 --> 00:08:21,859
What else would you like to hear about?

139
00:08:22,309 --> 00:08:24,109
Chebyshev's proof?

140
00:08:24,559 --> 00:08:27,759
The proof of the Prime Number Theorem

141
00:08:27,909 --> 00:08:32,109
and its connection to Riemann's zeta
function and the Riemann Hypothesis?

142
00:08:32,559 --> 00:08:37,340
Maybe a fun heuristic for why
the theorem should be true?

143
00:08:37,790 --> 00:08:39,990
Or maybe something else entirely?

144
00:08:40,440 --> 00:08:42,240
Tell me in the comments.

